=== Git in IntelliJ / PyCharm / WebStorm / PhpStorm / RubyMine

(((JetBrains)))
JetBrains IDEs (such as IntelliJ IDEA, PyCharm, WebStorm, PhpStorm, RubyMine, and others) ship with a Git Integration plugin.
It provides a dedicated view in the IDE to work with Git and GitHub Pull Requests.

.Version Control ToolWindow in JetBrains IDEs
image::images/jb.png[Version Control ToolWindow in JetBrains IDEs]

The integration relies on the command-line Git client, and requires one to be installed.
The official documentation is available at https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/using-git-integration.html[^].

===== Reading the IDE's Git state

The Version Control window presents the same three places Git uses: `HEAD` names the checked-out branch and commit, the index contains the next-commit snapshot, and the working tree contains the files currently on disk.
A staged edit can therefore differ from a second, unstaged edit to that same file.
Before making a commit, compare these states rather than treating the IDE's change list as one undifferentiated set.

[source]
----
Before: HEAD -> main (C); index = tree(C); working tree = tree(C)
Stage notes.txt after adding a line, then edit it again:
After:  HEAD -> main (C); index = notes.txt v2; working tree = notes.txt v3
----

The branch and commit do not move until a commit succeeds.
In the IDE, the staged area should show the snapshot prepared for the commit, while the unstaged area shows later edits.
You can confirm the same facts in the terminal with `git symbolic-ref --short HEAD`, `git rev-parse HEAD`, `git write-tree`, and `git status --short`.
